---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const join = (path: string) => `${baseUrl.replace(/\/$/, '')}${path}`;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="verify-user-authorization"
  description="Verify whether a user is authorized for a LINQ ERP tenant. Reads DynamoDB directly and mirrors the HarmonyAuthAuthorize endpoint logic."
  eyebrow="Tutorial"
  lede="After this page, you will be able to check whether a user is authorized for a LINQ ERP tenant — including the exact status enum and raw DynamoDB records — from the command line."
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: join('/') },
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: join('/tutorials/') },
    { label: 'verify-user-authorization' },
  ]}
>
  <Callout variant="info" label="TL;DR">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What it does:</strong> Reproduces the LINQ ERP <code>HarmonyAuthAuthorize</code> C# endpoint's decision logic locally, reading <code>erp_users</code> and <code>erp_tenants</code> from DynamoDB and returning a structured authorization envelope.</li>
      <li><strong>When to reach for it:</strong> A user gets a 401 after Auth0 login, an operator needs to confirm ERP tenant access, or an incident agent needs the raw DynamoDB records as evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>The one command to know:</strong> <code>python3 skills/verify-user-authorization/scripts/verify_authorization.py --tenant-id acme-isd --user-email alice@example.com --environment dev</code></li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="warn" label="Production safety">
    <p>
      When running against production, you <strong>must</strong> pass <code>--i-understand-this-is-prod</code>.
      The script refuses to run without it — there is no shorter alias by design. Before any DynamoDB call,
      the script prints an audit banner to stderr showing the resolved AWS profile, account ID, ARN, and
      table names. Check that banner before trusting the results.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="when-to-use">When to use this skill</h2>
  <p>
    Use verify-user-authorization when you need to answer "is this user authorized for tenant X?"
    with the same logic the production <code>HarmonyAuthAuthorize</code> C# endpoint uses. The skill
    reads the <code>erp_users</code> and <code>erp_tenants</code> DynamoDB tables directly and mirrors
    the endpoint's decision order — including two behaviors that may surprise you: a superuser row
    that exists but is inactive prevents the user-in-tenant row from being consulted at all, and a
    missing tenant row does <em>not</em> invalidate authorization (only an inactive tenant does).
  </p>
  <p>
    This skill is <strong>read-only</strong>. It never mutates ERP state. Production runs are
    supported but require the explicit <code>--i-understand-this-is-prod</code> flag; production
    CI runs are not supported without a pre-approved, named workflow.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Python 3 with <code>boto3</code> installed (<code>pip install boto3</code>).</li>
    <li>
      An AWS SSO session configured. Add the following block to <code>~/.aws/config</code> once,
      replacing the placeholder account IDs with the real LINQ account IDs from the AWS access portal:
      <pre><code>{`[sso-session linq]
sso_start_url = https://linq.awsapps.com/start
sso_region    = us-east-1
sso_registration_scopes = sso:account:access

[profile linq-erp-dev]
sso_session    = linq
sso_account_id = 111111111111
sso_role_name  = ERPDevReadOnly
region         = us-east-1

[profile linq-erp-prod]
sso_session    = linq
sso_account_id = 333333333333
sso_role_name  = ERPProdReadOnly
region         = us-east-1`}</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>
      Log in once per day:
      <pre><code>aws sso login --sso-session linq</code></pre>
      One login covers all profiles that reference <code>sso-session linq</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The skill derives its profile from <code>--environment</code>: <code>linq-erp-dev</code> for
      dev, <code>linq-erp-prod</code> for prod. Use <code>--aws-profile &lt;name&gt;</code> only for
      break-glass or incident-response overrides. Pass <code>--aws-profile ''</code> (empty string)
      to skip named profiles and fall back to boto3's default credential chain (Lambda role, GHA
      OIDC, instance profile).
    </li>
    <li>
      Optional environment variables: <code>AWS_REGION</code> (default: <code>us-east-1</code>),
      <code>LINQ_ERP_AWS_PROFILE</code> (workflow-level profile override),
      <code>LINQ_AWS_USE_AMBIENT_CHAIN=1</code> (skip named-profile resolution),
      <code>ERP_USERS_TABLE_NAME</code>, <code>ERP_TENANTS_TABLE_NAME</code> (table-name overrides
      for tests — default values are derived from <code>--environment</code>).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="walkthrough">Walkthrough</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Invoke against dev (the 99% case):</strong></p>
      <pre><code>python3 skills/verify-user-authorization/scripts/verify_authorization.py \
  --tenant-id acme-isd \
  --user-email alice@example.com \
  --environment dev</code></pre>
      <p>
        Before any DynamoDB call, the script prints an audit banner to stderr with the resolved
        profile, account ID, ARN, and table names. This is your per-invocation sanity check.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Invoke against prod:</strong></p>
      <pre><code>python3 skills/verify-user-authorization/scripts/verify_authorization.py \
  --tenant-id acme-isd \
  --user-email alice@example.com \
  --environment prod \
  --i-understand-this-is-prod</code></pre>
      <p>The script refuses to run without <code>--i-understand-this-is-prod</code> when targeting prod.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Read the response.</strong> Three fields to check first:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>authorization.authorized</code> — <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li>
        <li><code>authorization.status</code> — the enum that explains <em>why</em> (see the status table in <a href="#output-shape">Output shape</a>).</li>
        <li><code>matched_user_record</code> — <code>"in_tenant"</code>, <code>"superuser"</code>, or <code>null</code>. When <code>"superuser"</code>, the <code>user</code> field carries the superuser row, not the tenant row.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Common follow-ups.</strong> If the result is <code>TENANT_MISSING_BUT_USER_AUTHORIZED</code>, surface this loudly — it is a real but surprising authorized state per the C# logic. To ingest the investigation findings into the knowledge base, use <a href={join('/tutorials/kb-ingest/')}>kb-ingest</a>.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Callout variant="self-check" label="Did it work?">
    <ul>
      <li>An audit banner appeared on stderr before any DynamoDB call, showing profile, account ID, and table names.</li>
      <li>A JSON envelope appeared on stdout with <code>authorization.authorized</code> set to <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li>
      <li>The script exited 0 — it always exits 0; the envelope is the only contract.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="output-shape">Output shape</h2>
  <p>
    The script writes one JSON object to stdout. Sensitive <code>*_id</code> attributes are redacted
    by default; pass <code>--include-sensitive</code> only when the operator has explicitly asked
    for raw values.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "authorization": {
    "authorized": true,
    "status": "AUTHORIZED_USER",
    "reason": null
  },
  "user": {
    "PK": "#USRID#alice@example.com",
    "SK": "#TEN#acme-isd",
    "status": "active",
    "db_user_id": "<redacted>"
  },
  "tenant": {
    "PK": "#TEN#acme-isd",
    "SK": "#TEN#",
    "status": "active",
    "db_id": "<redacted>",
    "connection_string_id": "<redacted>"
  },
  "matched_user_record": "in_tenant"
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>Status enum values:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>AUTHORIZED_SUPERUSER</code>:</strong> Active superuser row and active tenant.</li>
    <li><strong><code>AUTHORIZED_USER</code>:</strong> No superuser row; active user-in-tenant row; active tenant.</li>
    <li><strong><code>SUPERUSER_DISABLED</code>:</strong> Superuser row exists but is inactive — user-in-tenant not consulted (mirrors C# behavior).</li>
    <li><strong><code>USER_DISABLED</code>:</strong> No superuser row; user-in-tenant exists but is inactive.</li>
    <li><strong><code>USER_NOT_FOUND</code>:</strong> Neither user row exists.</li>
    <li><strong><code>TENANT_DISABLED</code>:</strong> Tenant exists but is inactive; overrides user-side authorization.</li>
    <li><strong><code>TENANT_MISSING_BUT_USER_AUTHORIZED</code>:</strong> Tenant row absent but user/superuser is active — authorized per C#. Surface this loudly.</li>
    <li><strong><code>TENANT_MISSING_USER_NOT_AUTHORIZED</code>:</strong> Tenant row absent and user-side decision is also unauthorized.</li>
    <li><strong><code>ERROR</code>:</strong> AWS credential, network, or input error. See <code>reason</code> for detail.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On unauthorized outcomes, <code>user</code> and <code>tenant</code> are <code>null</code> to avoid leaking partial records.</p>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>Could not resolve AWS identity ... aws sso login --sso-session linq</code>:</strong>
      Your SSO token has expired or you have never logged in. Run <code>aws sso login --sso-session linq</code> to refresh it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>AWS profile 'linq-erp-prod' not found in ~/.aws/config</code>:</strong>
      Add the <code>[profile linq-erp-prod]</code> block shown in Prerequisites. The error message names the exact stanza to add.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>AccessDenied: ... is not authorized to perform: dynamodb:GetItem</code>:</strong>
      Your SSO permission set is missing the required role. Ask Operations to grant
      <code>ERPDevReadOnly</code> or <code>ERPProdReadOnly</code> for your user.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>Refusing prod run without --i-understand-this-is-prod</code>:</strong>
      Add the flag. It confirms explicit prod intent and has no shorter alias by design.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong account ID in the stderr audit banner:</strong>
      The profile is pointing at the wrong account. Re-check <code>sso_account_id</code> in
      <code>~/.aws/config</code> for the profile shown in the banner.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="where-this-fits">Where this fits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pillar:</strong> <a href={join('/how-it-works/')}>Skills management</a></li>
    <li><strong>Decision:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0016-aws-multi-account-skill-credentials.md">0016 — AWS multi-account skill credentials</a> (named-profile convention, audit banner, prod-acknowledgment rule, and three-phase error model)</li>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md"><code>skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md</code></a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="next">Try the next skill</h2>
  <p>
    After completing an authorization check, use
    <a href={join('/tutorials/kb-ingest/')}>kb-ingest</a> to add your findings — or any referenced
    documentation — to the LINQ knowledge base so future agents and engineers can retrieve them
    without re-running the investigation.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
